{
 "id": "STR-03",
 "name": "loop-bounds",
 "version": 2,
 "title": "Loops with variable bounds",
 "summary": "A water-intake tracker whose glasses come from two loops bounded by host variables: filled glasses from 0 to min(drunk, goal), empty outlines from drunk to goal; an empty range simply draws nothing.",
 "prompt": "A water tracker widget: a row of glasses for a daily goal of 8, the 5 already drunk filled blue and the rest as outlines, \"5 of 8 glasses\", a progress bar and \"3 to go\".",
 "level": 2,
 "family": "STR",
 "components": [
  "ProgressBar"
 ],
 "hand_built": "The glasses are drawn in a canvas loop because variable loop bounds are the lesson; the progress bar under them is a library ProgressBar.",
 "capabilities": {
  "primary": [
   "struct.loop"
  ],
  "secondary": [
   "data.loop",
   "data.expression",
   "text.dynamic",
   "text.anchor",
   "draw.shapes",
   "draw.stroke",
   "host.variables"
  ]
 },
 "use_cases": [
  "fitness",
  "widget",
  "control"
 ],
 "form_factor": "widget-4x2",
 "keywords": [
  "water tracker",
  "habit",
  "goal",
  "n of m",
  "rating",
  "filled and empty",
  "segments",
  "repeat n times",
  "dynamic count",
  "loop until variable"
 ],
 "teaches": [
  "Repeat a drawing a variable number of times: loop until \"min(@drunk, @goal)\" (an expression bound is re-read whenever the variable changes)",
  "Start a second loop where the first stopped (from \"@drunk\" until \"@goal\") to draw the remainder in another style",
  "Rely on empty ranges instead of if: when from >= until the loop body never runs (0 drunk: no filled glasses; goal reached: no outlines)",
  "Show the same ratio with a library ProgressBar under the canvas (\"progress\": \"@drunk / @goal\"): it clamps a value past the goal to a full bar and eases its fill when the variable changes"
 ],
 "pitfalls": [
  "Clamp a bound that can exceed the space you drew for: min(@drunk, @goal) keeps a ninth glass off the row",
  "Set the paint before the loop when every iteration shares it; a paint inside the body is re-applied on every iteration",
  "The index is a float: from \"@drunk\" = 4.5 gives 4.5, 5.5, …; floor the bound if the variable can be fractional",
  "Space loop items by a pitch derived from the width and the item count, g = (componentWidth() - 40) / @goal, rather than a literal 40: a fixed pitch overflows a narrow host and leaves a gap on a wide one"
 ],
 "snippets": [
  {
   "pointer": "/root/column/children/0/canvas/commands/6",
   "shows": "struct.loop",
   "note": "filled glasses: loop i from 0 until min(@drunk, @goal)"
  },
  {
   "pointer": "/root/column/children/0/canvas/commands/8",
   "shows": "struct.loop",
   "note": "outlines: loop i from @drunk until @goal"
  }
 ],
 "inputs": {
  "variables": {
   "drunk": {
    "type": "float",
    "meaning": "glasses drunk today",
    "default": 5,
    "min": 0,
    "max": 12,
    "step": 1
   },
   "goal": {
    "type": "float",
    "meaning": "daily goal in glasses (8 fit the row)",
    "default": 8,
    "min": 1,
    "max": 8,
    "step": 1
   }
  },
  "clock": null,
  "touch": false
 },
 "check": {
  "describe": "Five filled glasses then three outlines, \"5 of 8 glasses\", the bar at 62 %, \"3 to go\". drunk 0 gives eight outlines; drunk 9 gives eight filled glasses and \"0 to go\". Resized (narrow 240, wide 540) the row of glasses spans the canvas: the pitch is g = (componentWidth() - 40) / @goal and each glass is gw = min(28, 0.7 g) wide, so the goal's glasses always fill the row; the bar and the count follow the width.",
